1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's all around us, so much a part of our lives,

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
we forget it's there, but just try to survive a single day

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
without packaging. Virtually everything produced

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
on Earth needs to be packaged. We've taken great pains to make

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
sure that it gets up there in one piece. To sell it, ship it,

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
protect it, or keep it fresh, high-tech companies you never

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
knew existed invest billions to design every box,

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,040
every box, bottle, and bag you encountered,

9
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:37,300
and to crank them out by the ton to meet our insatiable demand.

10
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:38,740
This isn't rocket science.

11
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:39,940
It's harder.

12
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,140
And you'll never believe how much of every dollar

13
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:45,760
you spend pays for the cost of it all.

14
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:50,260
Now, the untold story of packaging on Modern Marvels.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:11,540
Packaging is the multi-billion dollar industry we all take for

16
00:01:11,541 --> 00:01:16,240
granted, despite the fact that we rely on it every day.

17
00:01:16,241 --> 00:01:19,100
One glance down the aisle at your local grocery store says it

18
00:01:19,101 --> 00:01:22,800
all. Every item on every shelf is in

19
00:01:22,801 --> 00:01:27,000
a package, and the variety is endless.

20
00:01:27,001 --> 00:01:29,800
There's an amazingly rich story behind the package of every

21
00:01:29,801 --> 00:01:32,300
product you buy.

22
00:01:32,301 --> 00:01:37,100
And you probably didn't even know you're a huge part of it.

23
00:01:37,101 --> 00:01:40,340
One dollar of every 11 you spend pays for the cost of

24
00:01:40,341 --> 00:01:42,340
packaging.

25
00:01:42,341 --> 00:01:45,760
And one third of everything you throw away consists of

26
00:01:45,761 --> 00:01:48,760
packaging materials.

27
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:55,900
Researchers at Michigan State University have long acknowledged

28
00:01:55,901 --> 00:01:58,600
truth that most of us ignore.

29
00:01:58,601 --> 00:02:00,500
Look at the blue one.

30
00:02:00,501 --> 00:02:03,900
Packaging is a science, a combination of design

31
00:02:03,901 --> 00:02:06,200
and engineering.

32
00:02:06,201 --> 00:02:09,980
Since 1952, the School of Packaging at Michigan State

33
00:02:09,981 --> 00:02:14,160
has set the tone and purpose for the industry.

34
00:02:14,161 --> 00:02:15,960
Packaging serves a whole bunch of functions.

35
00:02:15,961 --> 00:02:17,920
One's to contain a product.

36
00:02:17,921 --> 00:02:20,320
Another is to protect and preserve that product

37
00:02:20,321 --> 00:02:23,260
from all kinds of assaults.

38
00:02:23,261 --> 00:02:26,560
It's got to help the consumer use the product.

39
00:02:26,561 --> 00:02:28,460
It's got to be produced efficiently.

40
00:02:28,461 --> 00:02:31,000
And it's got to be environmentally friendly.

41
00:02:31,001 --> 00:02:33,940
That's a pretty tall order for a single substance,

42
00:02:33,941 --> 00:02:36,540
a single entity to do.

43
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:39,760
The experts here know that no matter what the material,

44
00:02:39,761 --> 00:02:42,400
the same goal applies.

45
00:02:42,401 --> 00:02:45,340
Produce the best container at the lowest cost,

46
00:02:45,341 --> 00:02:48,880
using the least amount of material.

47
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:52,080
Consider that deceptively simple form of packaging,

48
00:02:52,081 --> 00:02:56,320
the plastic water bottle. The water inside can only vary so

49
00:02:56,321 --> 00:03:00,580
much from brand to brand. So a staggering amount of money and

50
00:03:00,581 --> 00:03:05,480
manpower goes into the container. According to the National Resources

51
00:03:05,481 --> 00:03:11,440
Defense Council, at least 90% of the cost of bottled water covers things other than the

52
00:03:11,441 --> 00:03:15,960
water itself, including packaging, marketing, and shipping.

53
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:20,060
Americans go through 50 billion bottles of water annually.

54
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:26,880
And to design them, many of the beverage industry's major players

55
00:03:26,881 --> 00:03:32,080
turn to Amcor Rigid Plastics of Manchester, Michigan.

56
00:03:32,081 --> 00:03:37,080
On the surface, packaging a drink seems like a no-brainer.

57
00:03:37,081 --> 00:03:40,080
But designing the bottle is more complicated than you'd ever

58
00:03:40,081 --> 00:03:46,080
imagine. One reason why is they have to solve a vexing problem.

59
00:03:46,081 --> 00:03:49,080
And the answer, oddly enough, comes straight out of your

60
00:03:49,081 --> 00:03:53,880
out of your vegetable garden. The problem is that plastic bottles

61
00:03:53,881 --> 00:03:57,720
can easily deform shortly after they're filled.

62
00:03:57,721 --> 00:04:05,880
A liquid entering a bottle during the bottling process has been heated to 185 degrees Fahrenheit for pasteurization.

63
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:12,120
As it cools, it creates a low-pressure vacuum that causes the bottle to contract.

64
00:04:12,121 --> 00:04:17,800
Amcor's solution is hiding diaphragms in the walls and base that absorb the vacuum.

65
00:04:17,801 --> 00:04:19,960
The inspiration for the design?

66
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:21,800
The inside of a bell pepper.

67
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:27,140
We're actually working with the vacuum.

68
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:30,740
That vacuum itself is helping to form this new geometry.

69
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:32,740
And because these are new technologies

70
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:34,760
and a new way of looking at absorbing the vacuum,

71
00:04:34,860 --> 00:04:38,760
it naturally is giving us forms that we've not had before.

72
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:41,060
When the design is complete, engineers

73
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:43,560
test it in a virtual world.

74
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:46,200
It's like crash testing a bottle in a computer.

75
00:04:46,201 --> 00:04:51,760
Everything that that bottle is going to experience in the real world, like top load when it's being filled or stacked,

76
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:58,800
and also the vacuum or perhaps even internal pressure like in a soda bottle, we're going to go ahead and test all of that in the computer.

77
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:07,120
Competition in the bottled beverage industry is fierce, and the look of the packaging can have a huge impact on consumer preferences.

78
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:14,620
So graphic designers create virtual store shelves to show how the bottles will appear on display.

79
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:23,200
Designers can also get a feel for how the bottle fits in your hand before it's ever made.

80
00:05:23,201 --> 00:05:28,200
We can simulate how a real hand of a person is going to grab this bottle.

81
00:05:28,201 --> 00:05:32,200
We have hands we can move, fingers we can move.

82
00:05:32,201 --> 00:05:37,200
We can see where the contact points on the bottle versus the hand.

83
00:05:37,201 --> 00:05:39,200
All of our products do have to function.

84
00:05:39,201 --> 00:05:44,200
That's the most important piece of it is that it functions and serves that purpose to protect the product

85
00:05:44,201 --> 00:05:46,200
product and stand up to all the conditions.

86
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:48,340
It'll go through from manufacturing to the store

87
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:52,000
shelf and onto the consumer's home.

88
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:53,840
After the virtual bottle satisfies

89
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:58,040
Amcor's packaging brain trust, a 3D printer churns out a

90
00:05:58,140 --> 00:06:01,160
prototype.

91
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:03,960
The printhead makes several hundred passes over a volume

92
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:08,960
of plastic powder, and in each pass, sprays a binding solution

93
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:13,000
in the shape of one thin cross section of the bottle.

94
00:06:13,001 --> 00:06:17,800
Layer by layer, a solid form takes shape.

95
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:20,900
And after three hours, the prototype is complete.

96
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:29,440
Next, digital scanners analyze the bottle's dimensions to

97
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:32,960
confirm that it measures up to the design.

98
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:35,160
We'll do dimensional verifications to make sure that

99
00:06:35,260 --> 00:06:37,900
on the manufacturing side, we've got what we need.

100
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,760
And we'll finish polishing this model up and send it off to the

101
00:06:40,761 --> 00:06:43,600
for final aesthetic approval. Once we've got final approval

102
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:46,500
on the aesthetics, we're off to making bottles.

103
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,900
And those bottles are made from a plastic

104
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,500
called polyethylene terephthalate, also known as PET.

105
00:06:56,600 --> 00:07:01,620
Light, flexible, and durable. It was patented in 1973

106
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:06,020
and did for water what the pop-top can had done for soda.

107
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:10,760
It turned water into a drink we could enjoy anywhere, anytime.

108
00:07:10,761 --> 00:07:16,760
But our devotion to bottled water comes at a high environmental price.

109
00:07:16,761 --> 00:07:23,760
PET is made from petroleum, and more than 17 million barrels of oil are required to produce

110
00:07:23,761 --> 00:07:28,760
the 30 billion bottles purchased by Americans every year.

111
00:07:28,761 --> 00:07:35,760
Since PET is non-biodegradable, environmentalists advocate aggressive recycling efforts.

112
00:07:35,761 --> 00:07:39,260
But nearly 8 out of 10 plastic water bottles end up in a

113
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:44,860
landfill. Also, as consumers, we need to

114
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:49,200
consider how packaging water contributes to its cost.

115
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:53,720
A bottle of water costing $1.35 could be refilled with tap water

116
00:07:53,820 --> 00:08:00,720
every day for 10 years for less than the same price.

117
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:04,820
There's more eye-opening facts about the packaging of our food.

118
00:08:04,821 --> 00:08:06,860
The demands of 21st century consumers

119
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:09,160
have gone beyond preservation.

120
00:08:09,260 --> 00:08:11,560
Now we require convenience.

121
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:14,460
It's not enough that containers keep food fresh.

122
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:16,060
They must now help us cook it.

123
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:21,580
One company answering the challenge

124
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:25,380
is the Sealed Air Corporation, a global packaging innovator

125
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:27,880
for 50 years.

126
00:08:27,980 --> 00:08:31,780
Its product line called Cryovac seals, protects, and actually

127
00:08:31,781 --> 00:08:34,680
helps cook fresh and frozen food.

128
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:38,380
It works for both fresh and frozen applications.

129
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:40,320
It's steam-assisted cooking.

130
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:42,820
When the product is ready, you peel it open,

131
00:08:42,920 --> 00:08:45,720
and you can actually eat directly from the package.

132
00:08:45,820 --> 00:08:47,420
Other innovations take the guesswork

133
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:50,640
out of measuring, marinate on demand,

134
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:55,540
and make novice chefs look like experts.

135
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:59,040
The Cryovac material may look like a simple plastic wrap,

136
00:08:59,041 --> 00:09:01,440
but it's made from a patented formula.

137
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:03,640
It's comprised of different layers.

138
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:06,980
Some keep oxygen and bacteria out.

139
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:10,080
Others keep moisture and flavor in.

140
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:12,280
Others distribute microwave heat evenly.

141
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:18,700
Cryovac is a high-tech descendant of one of packaging's

142
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:21,300
milestone innovations.

143
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:25,940
Cellophane, the ubiquitous transparent film made from plant

144
00:09:25,941 --> 00:09:29,340
called cellulose, has been a staple of food preservation

145
00:09:29,341 --> 00:09:33,140
since 1913. And like many great inventions,

146
00:09:33,141 --> 00:09:37,180
it was created completely by accident.

147
00:09:37,181 --> 00:09:40,780
Credit a Swiss textile engineer whose real intention was to

148
00:09:40,781 --> 00:09:44,700
invent a stain-proof tablecloth. He discovered that the

149
00:09:44,701 --> 00:09:49,300
cellulose he tried to apply to the cloth kept peeling off as

150
00:09:49,301 --> 00:09:53,140
the clear, water-resistant, biodegradable film we know

151
00:09:53,141 --> 00:09:58,980
we know today. It paved the way for innovations like Cryovac,

152
00:09:58,981 --> 00:10:02,180
which is formed into bags that not only help packagers preserve

153
00:10:02,181 --> 00:10:06,980
our food, but also lend a helping hand to prepare it.

154
00:10:06,981 --> 00:10:10,100
The machine behind me is one of the main components of our

155
00:10:10,101 --> 00:10:13,220
packaging process. The product will go into a chamber

156
00:10:13,221 --> 00:10:16,020
system and then that chamber will evacuate the air out of the

157
00:10:16,021 --> 00:10:20,100
package and then we'll make a closing seal. With today's

158
00:10:20,101 --> 00:10:23,700
we're able to completely automate that operation

159
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:26,900
from ready to pack till it's ready to ship

160
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
to a food processor or a consumer.

161
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:37,760
No country consumes more bottled water per person

162
00:10:37,860 --> 00:10:39,060
than Italy.

163
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:43,160
Each Italian drinks an average of 55 gallons per year,

164
00:10:43,260 --> 00:10:45,060
nearly twice that of each American.

165
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:55,100
In recent years, a new packaging term has entered

166
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:58,500
the American lexicon, rap rage.

167
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:02,540
Each year, 6,000 Americans wind up in emergency rooms

168
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:06,860
with injuries suffered while trying to open packaging.

169
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:10,660
The most common source of rap rage is the clamshell,

170
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:14,400
that plastic monstrosity made of two rigid halves sealed

171
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:17,360
together with ultrasonic welding.

172
00:11:17,460 --> 00:11:20,080
There's sort of a love-hate relationship with the

173
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:23,140
package because people love it because they know that they can

174
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:25,400
see the product that they're buying and know that all the

175
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:27,940
parts are inside, and the retailers like it because it's

176
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:30,140
hard to steal. People hate it because it's very

177
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:32,540
difficult to open with scissors, and anyone who has tried

178
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:36,340
understands what I'm talking about. The cry of consumer

179
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:39,160
protest has reached many packaging companies that are

180
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:44,660
riding to our rescue. L.A.'s Impact Manufacturing and

181
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:48,660
Winterborne Corporation offer a frustration-free alternative they

182
00:11:48,661 --> 00:11:53,260
called the EnviroShell. A large portion of it isn't

183
00:11:53,261 --> 00:11:57,100
plastic, but paper, which makes it a breeze to open.

184
00:11:57,101 --> 00:12:00,300
Well, what we're looking at here is the machine that seals

185
00:12:00,301 --> 00:12:03,300
what we call a trap blister. Unlike the clamshell that was

186
00:12:03,301 --> 00:12:06,620
mostly plastic, a trap blister is mostly paper, making it more

187
00:12:06,621 --> 00:12:09,340
sustainable. How it works is that the paper

188
00:12:09,341 --> 00:12:11,980
has a special coating that is heat activated.

189
00:12:11,981 --> 00:12:16,060
The product is put into a PET blister placed inside the paper

190
00:12:16,061 --> 00:12:19,300
trapping it on the flanges. And then it is sent to this

191
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:23,800
machine over here, where heat is passed through, activating

192
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:27,800
the coating on the paper, sealing it together, giving us

193
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:33,720
a nice, solid, sealed package. Many Envirochelle packages

194
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:38,120
are oversized, designed to not only protect contents, but also

195
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:41,220
act as billboards to lure buyers.

196
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:44,320
Making them requires not one, but three production

197
00:12:44,321 --> 00:12:48,960
production facilities to produce the plastic, the paper, and the

198
00:12:48,961 --> 00:12:54,960
printing. First up, the plastic.

199
00:12:54,961 --> 00:12:58,360
The process starts with a roll of film that's 20 mil thick.

200
00:12:58,361 --> 00:13:01,080
It comes through this section here and gets fed into the

201
00:13:01,081 --> 00:13:03,480
heater bank. The heater bank will heat it to

202
00:13:03,481 --> 00:13:07,280
approximately 185 degrees, making the plastic very

203
00:13:07,281 --> 00:13:10,280
pliable. It then goes into the forming

204
00:13:10,281 --> 00:13:13,480
section. There's an aluminum mold that is the shape of the

205
00:13:13,481 --> 00:13:16,520
package that we desire. The plastic is heated.

206
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:20,260
It goes into the vacuum chamber and gets sucked down by a vacuum

207
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:24,560
mounted underneath the tool and then cooled quickly to form it

208
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:26,100
in the shape of the package.

209
00:13:29,860 --> 00:13:33,300
Making the paper comes next at LA Corps,

210
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:36,840
also in Los Angeles. Although the paper is the key

211
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:40,440
to making the EnviroShell easier to open, it still needs to be

212
00:13:40,441 --> 00:13:42,840
strong enough to endure shipping.

213
00:13:42,940 --> 00:13:45,840
So the paper created here is corrugated,

214
00:13:45,940 --> 00:13:48,940
meaning it's folded into ridges that reinforce the material.

215
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:56,880
LA Corp corrugates 700 miles of paper each day.

216
00:13:56,980 --> 00:14:00,760
What we see here is the medium going through the corrugating

217
00:14:00,860 --> 00:14:02,400
roll and coming out.

218
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:06,740
If you'll notice, the medium is being heated with steam so that

219
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:08,700
whenever it goes through the corrugating roll,

220
00:14:08,701 --> 00:14:11,700
it's more flexible and it won't tear out whenever it gets inside

221
00:14:11,701 --> 00:14:15,700
the corrugating roll. As the production line turns out

222
00:14:15,701 --> 00:14:19,700
layer after layer of corrugated paper, machines bond them

223
00:14:19,701 --> 00:14:22,700
together to make panels rigid enough to protect the products

224
00:14:22,701 --> 00:14:26,700
they'll contain. We've got the liner, medium,

225
00:14:26,701 --> 00:14:31,700
liner, medium, and then the final liner being applied. All of

226
00:14:31,701 --> 00:14:36,700
that is being applied with a thin layer of glue. It's going to

227
00:14:36,701 --> 00:14:39,040
It's going to be all combined right here at this point.

228
00:14:39,140 --> 00:14:41,220
Go through the hot plate section for curing.

229
00:14:45,740 --> 00:14:50,600
From start to finish, computers monitor every aspect of the production process.

230
00:14:55,940 --> 00:15:03,260
And workers perform quality control checks to make sure the final product is ready for the last step in making the EnviroShel.

231
00:15:03,261 --> 00:15:10,100
That happens here at LA's Fleetwood Fiber, where one of the world's most sophisticated

232
00:15:10,101 --> 00:15:13,080
printers applies colored inks to the corrugated paper.

233
00:15:13,081 --> 00:15:20,080
It's a seven-color corrugated high graphics printer and die cutter, all in line in one

234
00:15:20,081 --> 00:15:22,360
pass. The sheets are automatically unloaded.

235
00:15:22,361 --> 00:15:26,851
They're automatically fed into the up stacker. They go into the printing unit.

236
00:15:26,852 --> 00:15:27,600
The machine's

237
00:15:27,601 --> 00:15:32,400
capable of doing seven colors or six color and varnish.

238
00:15:32,401 --> 00:15:35,100
If I'm doing less than seven colors,

239
00:15:35,101 --> 00:15:38,640
I can actually go into the machine while it's printing

240
00:15:38,641 --> 00:15:40,980
and set up the next job.

241
00:15:40,981 --> 00:15:42,180
Everything prints above us.

242
00:15:45,560 --> 00:15:48,600
Prints upside down.

243
00:15:48,601 --> 00:15:50,560
Each month, the enormous printer

244
00:15:50,561 --> 00:15:54,220
consumes up to 20,000 pounds of ink and coatings,

245
00:15:54,221 --> 00:15:57,340
formulated in Fleetwood's own ink lab.

246
00:15:57,341 --> 00:16:02,740
Any given number of combinations of inks are all created here,

247
00:16:02,741 --> 00:16:06,640
tested here, and ultimately are saved in the computer so that

248
00:16:06,641 --> 00:16:08,680
when we run a job out in the plant,

249
00:16:08,681 --> 00:16:11,200
we can repeat that with the consistency

250
00:16:11,201 --> 00:16:14,480
that our customers expect.

251
00:16:14,481 --> 00:16:16,000
The rainbow of inks created here

252
00:16:16,001 --> 00:16:19,280
enables the mega printer to crank out up to 10,000 sheets

253
00:16:19,281 --> 00:16:21,840
per hour.

254
00:16:21,841 --> 00:16:25,780
Then it automatically die cuts the final pieces.

255
00:16:25,781 --> 00:16:29,040
What you're looking at here is our rotary die-cut die board.

256
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:36,180
In order for the die-cut to go cut the corrugated and not get stuck, we need to be rubbered up.

257
00:16:36,380 --> 00:16:38,640
So all these rubber pieces are like springs.

258
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:44,680
It comes through and it die-cuts the corrugated, it springs back, die-cuts the corrugated,

259
00:16:44,820 --> 00:16:46,900
and it just does that in a rotary fashion.

260
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:54,220
Colorful corrugated panels emerge from the printer, ready for their transformation into retail packaging.

261
00:16:55,780 --> 00:16:59,580
All that's left to do now is bring the paper and plastic

262
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:04,480
components together, and you've got to complete it in Viroshell.

263
00:17:04,580 --> 00:17:07,820
Move over, clamshell. This innovative package provides

264
00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:11,320
just as much protection without giving consumers trying to open

265
00:17:11,420 --> 00:17:17,440
it a nasty case of wrap rage. Half of all the paper produced

266
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:20,880
in the United States is used to wrap and decorate consumer

267
00:17:20,881 --> 00:17:26,180
consumer products. Paper comprises about 41% of our

268
00:17:26,181 --> 00:17:36,120
trash. The challenge of packaging generally

269
00:17:36,121 --> 00:17:41,740
grows as the thing you're trying to package gets bigger and bigger.

270
00:17:41,741 --> 00:17:44,740
No one knows this better than the experts of the Mammut

271
00:17:44,741 --> 00:17:48,680
Corporation of the Netherlands. They pack and ship everything

272
00:17:48,681 --> 00:17:53,920
from buildings to bridges. The company's name says it all.

273
00:17:54,020 --> 00:17:55,920
Mammut is Dutch for mammoth.

274
00:17:59,620 --> 00:18:02,420
We have the ability to pick up anything that the good Lord

275
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:05,220
didn't tie down to the earth. We can pick it up and we can

276
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:10,340
move it, sometimes set it into millimeters of where you need it.

277
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:14,440
At Mammut's US headquarters in Rocheron, Texas, workers are

278
00:18:14,441 --> 00:18:18,840
are tackling a colossal challenge, packaging one of the world's

279
00:18:18,841 --> 00:18:22,780
largest cranes for shipment overseas.

280
00:18:22,781 --> 00:18:28,040
The Platform Twin Rig Containerized Crane, PTC for short,

281
00:18:28,041 --> 00:18:31,480
stands as high as a 65-story skyscraper

282
00:18:31,481 --> 00:18:35,080
and weighs 2,100 metric tons.

283
00:18:35,081 --> 00:18:39,480
That's 4,200,000 pounds.

284
00:18:39,481 --> 00:18:42,580
Packing a Titan this size demands a lot more than a boatload

285
00:18:42,581 --> 00:18:47,580
The job will take an entire week to complete.

286
00:18:47,680 --> 00:18:51,680
As they package the giant crane, their work has been made more

287
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:54,520
manageable by its ingenious design.

288
00:18:54,620 --> 00:18:58,520
Many of its individual parts do double duty, acting as containers

289
00:18:58,620 --> 00:19:00,240
for other pieces.

290
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:03,140
When the crane is in action, steel panels like this one

291
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:06,980
function as counterweights for the enormous boom.

292
00:19:07,080 --> 00:19:10,480
Enabling the crane to lift three quarters of the crane

293
00:19:10,481 --> 00:19:15,480
enabling the crane to lift three-quarters of its own weight.

294
00:19:15,481 --> 00:19:18,480
But during packaging, the counterweights are bolted onto

295
00:19:18,481 --> 00:19:24,480
other sections of the crane to form boxes enclosing them.

296
00:19:24,481 --> 00:19:28,480
Many of the reconfigured packaged sections are either 20

297
00:19:28,481 --> 00:19:32,480
or 40 feet long, the exact dimensions of shipping containers

298
00:19:32,481 --> 00:19:35,480
that fit on commercial cargo vessels and tractor-trailer

299
00:19:35,481 --> 00:19:39,480
rigs. This makes transporting the sections

300
00:19:39,481 --> 00:19:44,480
year. Other sections, including those containing winches and

301
00:19:44,481 --> 00:19:48,480
other machinery, will be lifted directly onto trucks and shipped

302
00:19:48,481 --> 00:19:51,480
as is. This piece behind us will actually

303
00:19:51,481 --> 00:19:55,480
be bolted up like it sits. That way when we get it there and

304
00:19:55,481 --> 00:19:58,480
we get it bolted up, it's ready. Then all you have to do is

305
00:19:58,481 --> 00:20:02,480
spool things together and it's operational. Depending on the

306
00:20:02,481 --> 00:20:06,480
configuration required for a job, the crane fits in 80 to

307
00:20:06,481 --> 00:20:12,480
each weighing 20 to 30 tons. Moving these megaloads requires

308
00:20:12,481 --> 00:20:17,480
some heavy-duty vehicles, like this steerable trailer.

309
00:20:17,481 --> 00:20:22,480
These axles can steer 45-degree angles, so each one of these,

310
00:20:22,481 --> 00:20:25,480
when the truck goes and makes a turn and he wants to back up,

311
00:20:25,481 --> 00:20:29,480
we can steer it in whatever direction we want to go in.

312
00:20:29,481 --> 00:20:33,480
The heaviest containers of all hitch a ride on this 48-wheeled

313
00:20:33,481 --> 00:20:37,120
wheeled powerhouse called a self-propelled modular

314
00:20:37,220 --> 00:20:41,180
transporter. Each wheel can be individually

315
00:20:41,280 --> 00:20:44,120
controlled to evenly distribute the crushing weight.

316
00:20:50,240 --> 00:20:53,440
You've probably noticed there's no driver aboard.

317
00:20:53,540 --> 00:20:56,840
There he is, maneuvering the huge low rider through even the

318
00:20:56,940 --> 00:21:00,540
tightest quarters by remote control.

319
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:02,780
You can pick up a lot more weight with it versus one of

320
00:21:02,781 --> 00:21:03,780
of the other trucks.

321
00:21:03,781 --> 00:21:06,780
The way this one's set up is about 360 metric tons.

322
00:21:06,781 --> 00:21:10,780
It's about around about 32 tons per axle.

323
00:21:10,781 --> 00:21:13,780
Like all large-scale packaging jobs,

324
00:21:13,781 --> 00:21:15,780
tech plays a crucial role.

325
00:21:15,781 --> 00:21:18,780
But manpower is no less critical.

326
00:21:18,781 --> 00:21:21,780
All the technological advances we've made over the years

327
00:21:21,781 --> 00:21:25,780
in building stronger machines and more advanced equipment,

328
00:21:25,781 --> 00:21:27,780
it still boils down to the men and women

329
00:21:27,781 --> 00:21:29,780
that we have employed for us.

330
00:21:32,780 --> 00:21:39,780
One massive global packaging effort literally takes an army

331
00:21:39,781 --> 00:21:44,500
to get the job done. In fact, it takes an army, navy,

332
00:21:44,501 --> 00:21:48,500
marine corps, and air force. Meet the men and women of the U.S.

333
00:21:48,501 --> 00:21:52,860
Transportation Command, USTRANSCOM for short.

334
00:21:52,861 --> 00:21:56,600
Their gargantuan task is to pack and ship every single item

335
00:21:56,601 --> 00:22:01,180
required for all branches of the American military.

336
00:22:01,181 --> 00:22:04,780
U.S. Transcom is really responsible overall for the

337
00:22:04,781 --> 00:22:07,980
strategic movement of our military forces around the

338
00:22:07,981 --> 00:22:11,120
world. We basically do about 900 sorties a day,

339
00:22:11,121 --> 00:22:16,120
2,600 truck movements. We have 44 ships that are onloading,

340
00:22:16,121 --> 00:22:20,240
offloading, or underway, and about 100 real car shipments

341
00:22:20,241 --> 00:22:24,440
every day. If we actually went to full-out war, we'd do about

342
00:22:24,441 --> 00:22:28,080
three to four times that amount. U.S. Transcom

343
00:22:28,081 --> 00:22:32,320
headquarters is located at Scott Air Force Base in Illinois.

344
00:22:32,321 --> 00:22:37,160
Here 1,400 military and civilian personnel supervise the efforts

345
00:22:37,161 --> 00:22:41,760
of 145,000 workers worldwide.

346
00:22:41,761 --> 00:22:44,460
We have some folks who are planning missions for the air

347
00:22:44,461 --> 00:22:47,500
crews, filing flight plans, checking weather, notices to

348
00:22:47,501 --> 00:22:50,300
airmen. We have other personnel in here who are actually

349
00:22:50,301 --> 00:22:53,540
monitoring the missions, looking for problems, looking for choke

350
00:22:53,541 --> 00:22:56,400
points, solving crises before they happen.

351
00:22:56,401 --> 00:22:59,100
If this isn't rocket science, it's harder.

352
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:04,440
US Transcom manages more than 100,000 flights each year.

353
00:23:04,540 --> 00:23:09,640
Many originate here at Dover Air Force Base in Delaware.

354
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:14,760
Huge C-5 and C-17 cargo planes function as aerial moving vans

355
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:18,160
for all manner of weapons, vehicles, equipment, and

356
00:23:18,260 --> 00:23:19,660
supplies.

357
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:21,160
Our mission here at Dover Air Force Base

358
00:23:21,260 --> 00:23:22,160
is to deliver.

359
00:23:22,260 --> 00:23:26,400
Dover Air Force Base supplies approximately 75% of all cargo

360
00:23:26,401 --> 00:23:29,140
through the air transit, the strategic airlift system.

361
00:23:29,240 --> 00:23:31,200
We're the guys loading the aircraft,

362
00:23:31,300 --> 00:23:33,200
aggregating the cargo onto the pallets

363
00:23:33,300 --> 00:23:35,340
to get them over to the warfighter.

364
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:37,340
Every day, hundreds of cargo pallets

365
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:40,340
arrive at Dover from shippers.

366
00:23:40,440 --> 00:23:42,640
Each needs to be inspected, weighed, and packaged

367
00:23:42,740 --> 00:23:46,160
to protect the contents during flight.

368
00:23:46,260 --> 00:23:48,160
Certain aircraft have height limitations.

369
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:52,200
Some aircraft have contour limitations, weight limitations.

370
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:55,360
So we as cargo processors look at that daily

371
00:23:55,361 --> 00:23:58,160
before we build any pallet, we know what we're building it for.

372
00:23:58,960 --> 00:24:01,260
To maximize the use of cargo space,

373
00:24:01,960 --> 00:24:03,780
crews use computer-assisted lasers

374
00:24:03,781 --> 00:24:06,500
to analyze the size and shape of every pallet.

375
00:24:07,940 --> 00:24:11,380
Each pallet is labeled with a radio frequency identification tag

376
00:24:11,381 --> 00:24:13,400
so it can be tracked in transit.

377
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:17,780
On any given day, we'll have approximately 600 tons of cargo.

378
00:24:18,180 --> 00:24:20,180
That's a lot of stuff out there in the system,

379
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:23,460
and that's a lot of customers wanting to know where their stuff is.

380
00:24:23,461 --> 00:24:26,800
So every single pallet will have an RFID tag.

381
00:24:26,801 --> 00:24:29,360
After a pallet completes its packaging prep,

382
00:24:29,361 --> 00:24:31,600
it awaits its airborne journey in a state-of-the-art

383
00:24:31,601 --> 00:24:35,600
computerized warehouse.

384
00:24:35,601 --> 00:24:38,400
Cargo is organized by destination.

385
00:24:38,401 --> 00:24:41,320
A full plane load can be retrieved and ready to ship

386
00:24:41,321 --> 00:24:44,040
within two hours.

387
00:24:44,041 --> 00:24:47,120
Just prior to departure, pallets are moved to staging

388
00:24:47,121 --> 00:24:50,080
docks, where they're grouped to provide the most efficient use

389
00:24:50,081 --> 00:24:51,280
of space aboard the aircraft.

390
00:24:55,080 --> 00:24:58,620
Then servicemen operating vehicles called K-loaders

391
00:24:58,621 --> 00:25:00,420
roll into action.

392
00:25:00,421 --> 00:25:03,820
A K-loader can carry up to 60,000 pounds

393
00:25:03,821 --> 00:25:05,520
and load six pallets at a time.

394
00:25:15,140 --> 00:25:19,240
Inside the C-5 planes, the cargo is carefully positioned,

395
00:25:19,241 --> 00:25:25,780
ensuring that no space is wasted in the 121-foot long hold.

396
00:25:25,781 --> 00:25:29,240
The hold is wide enough to house an eight-lane bowling alley

397
00:25:29,241 --> 00:25:32,120
and roomy enough for six Greyhound buses.

398
00:25:32,121 --> 00:25:36,380
If I upload this piece of cargo that has a full up armor kit

399
00:25:36,381 --> 00:25:40,520
for Humvee on Friday, on that next Monday,

400
00:25:40,521 --> 00:25:42,000
that person over in Afghanistan is

401
00:25:42,001 --> 00:25:43,660
going to be putting onto his Humvee

402
00:25:43,661 --> 00:25:45,900
and drive away safely because of the pallet

403
00:25:45,901 --> 00:25:47,540
that I uploaded for him.

404
00:25:47,541 --> 00:25:49,580
We're very grateful for everything

405
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:51,440
that we can do to help the people that are over there

406
00:25:51,540 --> 00:25:54,180
fighting for us every day.

407
00:25:54,280 --> 00:25:57,080
Although shipping by air is fast and efficient,

408
00:25:57,180 --> 00:25:59,220
it's also costly.

409
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:01,780
So when the time factor is less critical,

410
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:06,460
US Transcom ships its cargo by land and sea.

411
00:26:06,560 --> 00:26:09,460
The Port of Baltimore is one of US Transcom's key packaging

412
00:26:09,560 --> 00:26:13,100
points for shipping cargo, including $30 million

413
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:14,500
Chinook helicopters.

414
00:26:17,540 --> 00:26:21,540
A packaging procedure once used extensively but now abandoned

415
00:26:21,640 --> 00:26:25,480
was to essentially shrink-wrap choppers to protect them

416
00:26:25,580 --> 00:26:26,280
during shipment.

417
00:26:29,380 --> 00:26:32,280
The shrink-wrap strategy has now been replaced by enclosing

418
00:26:32,380 --> 00:26:36,200
the choppers in a different type of container,

419
00:26:36,300 --> 00:26:39,660
the environmentally controlled cargo hold of a Navy sea lift

420
00:26:39,760 --> 00:26:44,660
ship like the USNS Mendoza.

421
00:26:44,661 --> 00:26:46,660
But before the choppers can be loaded,

422
00:26:46,760 --> 00:26:49,900
they need to be packaged for the trip.

423
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,200
The most important part of that effort

424
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:55,100
is removing and securing the rotor blades,

425
00:26:55,200 --> 00:26:59,600
which weigh between 350 and 600 pounds.

426
00:26:59,700 --> 00:27:02,320
It takes a lot of teamwork, a lot of communication,

427
00:27:02,420 --> 00:27:05,020
because you've got guys up top that are disconnecting things,

428
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:07,120
and guys down at the bottom that are actually holding

429
00:27:07,220 --> 00:27:08,520
the rotor blade in place.

430
00:27:08,620 --> 00:27:11,020
We have a man that operates our spider crane that lifts

431
00:27:11,120 --> 00:27:13,620
the blade cradle up to the blade, captures the blade,

432
00:27:13,621 --> 00:27:16,260
the blade, holds it while we disconnect all the main

433
00:27:16,261 --> 00:27:18,820
attaching components, and that's what we use to lower

434
00:27:18,821 --> 00:27:22,560
the blade down. The container that protects

435
00:27:22,561 --> 00:27:27,360
the blades in transit is the helicopter itself.

436
00:27:27,361 --> 00:27:29,560
The blades are very expensive. They're made out of a fiberglass

437
00:27:29,561 --> 00:27:32,580
composite material, and you have to be very careful when we're

438
00:27:32,581 --> 00:27:35,580
storing the blades and also with the removal of the port ops.

439
00:27:35,581 --> 00:27:40,080
It is a high visibility, high dollar operation.

440
00:27:40,081 --> 00:27:42,480
After the rotor blades are secure and the rotor head is

441
00:27:42,481 --> 00:27:46,480
is wrapped, the helicopters are lifted by crane onto the Mendoza

442
00:27:46,580 --> 00:27:48,080
and down into the cargo hold.

443
00:27:50,880 --> 00:27:53,820
Only one step remains for packaging and shipping the Chinook

444
00:27:53,920 --> 00:27:58,320
helicopters, tying them down to make certain rough seas don't

445
00:27:58,420 --> 00:28:01,240
move them during transit. On helicopters,

446
00:28:01,340 --> 00:28:03,660
we don't like to use chains because there's no flex

447
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:06,340
involved when the aircraft and or boat is moving while it's

448
00:28:06,440 --> 00:28:08,980
underway. It can actually cause a chain

449
00:28:09,080 --> 00:28:11,380
to actually pull out one of our tie-down points.

450
00:28:11,381 --> 00:28:13,520
So that's why we prefer to use straps.

451
00:28:13,521 --> 00:28:16,720
It gives a little flex.

452
00:28:16,721 --> 00:28:19,820
U.S. Transcom cargo ships like the Mendoza

453
00:28:19,821 --> 00:28:23,320
not only help keep America's military fully equipped,

454
00:28:23,321 --> 00:28:27,220
but also redefine our notions of packaging.

455
00:28:27,221 --> 00:28:30,740
As I tell General Petraeus or General Mattis

456
00:28:30,741 --> 00:28:32,540
and the other combatant commanders,

457
00:28:32,541 --> 00:28:34,740
my job is to make sure they never worry about this.

458
00:28:34,741 --> 00:28:36,480
I will get it to them when they need it,

459
00:28:36,481 --> 00:28:39,740
and if I got to speed things up, I'll take care of that.

460
00:28:41,380 --> 00:28:46,380
Packaging and transporting one Chinook helicopter aboard a C-5

461
00:28:46,480 --> 00:28:51,380
cargo jet costs more than $500,000.

462
00:28:56,380 --> 00:29:00,380
Nearly 10% of the price of any product is the cost of the

463
00:29:00,480 --> 00:29:01,380
packaging.

464
00:29:01,480 --> 00:29:05,380
But the big bucks you fork over for packaging don't stop there.

465
00:29:05,480 --> 00:29:10,380
We shell out almost $3 billion every year to package gifts.

466
00:29:11,380 --> 00:29:14,580
And when it's time to relocate, we spend $10 billion

467
00:29:14,680 --> 00:29:19,180
on packing services and products.

468
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:22,420
The average American uses 60 pounds of plastic packaging

469
00:29:22,520 --> 00:29:23,520
material every year.

470
00:29:27,020 --> 00:29:29,340
And much of it is this iconic brand.

471
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:37,680
Behold the marvel of bubble wrap, one of civilization's most

472
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:40,840
popular packaging brands that spawned a company now

473
00:29:40,841 --> 00:29:46,140
worth $4 billion. Our obsession to pop it makes it one of life's

474
00:29:46,141 --> 00:29:51,080
leading time wasters and an oddly effective stress reliever.

475
00:29:51,081 --> 00:29:55,580
It was invented in 1957, made of two shower curtains sealed

476
00:29:55,581 --> 00:30:01,200
together by entrepreneurs Alfred Fielding and Marc Chavons.

477
00:30:01,201 --> 00:30:05,500
They later started mass producing it on this machine.

478
00:30:05,501 --> 00:30:09,240
But believe it or not, it was all a mistake, since their real

479
00:30:09,241 --> 00:30:13,440
real goal was to make a funky new kind of wallpaper.

480
00:30:13,441 --> 00:30:16,740
They needed to create a textured padded wallpaper that

481
00:30:16,741 --> 00:30:20,580
would be laminated to wallpaper and to be applied to the wall

482
00:30:20,581 --> 00:30:23,840
that would give it a third dimension.

483
00:30:23,841 --> 00:30:26,280
The inventors quickly realized that they had an outstanding

484
00:30:26,281 --> 00:30:29,580
cushion material. Bubble wrap is now produced

485
00:30:29,581 --> 00:30:33,480
at sealed air production facilities around the world,

486
00:30:33,481 --> 00:30:37,700
including its birthplace in Saddlebrook, New Jersey.

487
00:30:37,701 --> 00:30:42,220
Bubble wrap actually starts from a process of various types of polyethylene resins.

488
00:30:42,480 --> 00:30:47,120
We mix these different ingredients up into our blender at the top up here.

489
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:52,180
There's various types of ingredients that are then forced down into our extruder at the lower level.

490
00:30:52,181 --> 00:30:58,020
We actually then extrude two layers of film that one of those layers will be used to form the bubble.

491
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:05,600
The machine that transforms the plastic film into bubble wrap has changed considerably in the past 50 years.

492
00:31:07,700 --> 00:31:09,860
as you watch it work its magic.

493
00:31:09,861 --> 00:31:11,740
Let's debunk them now.

494
00:31:11,741 --> 00:31:13,760
Most people think we're actually blowing air

495
00:31:13,761 --> 00:31:16,140
into the bubble to form the bubble,

496
00:31:16,141 --> 00:31:18,660
but it's actually a heated drum with a vacuum.

497
00:31:18,661 --> 00:31:21,000
So we're sucking the molten flat film down

498
00:31:21,001 --> 00:31:22,620
into the embossing cylinder.

499
00:31:22,621 --> 00:31:24,600
And then the other hot molten film

500
00:31:24,601 --> 00:31:27,380
is actually laminated to the bubble

501
00:31:27,381 --> 00:31:31,100
to trap the air that forms the actual bubble impression.

502
00:31:31,101 --> 00:31:33,700
And then it immediately travels over to the chill roller,

503
00:31:33,701 --> 00:31:36,720
where it's quickly cooled, and it forms the final impression

504
00:31:36,721 --> 00:31:41,120
as far as the bubble. Each bubble in bubble wrap is

505
00:31:41,121 --> 00:31:45,060
uniform in size, but the width and length of the rolls is

506
00:31:45,061 --> 00:31:48,760
determined by Sealed Air's clients. All of our bubble wrap

507
00:31:48,761 --> 00:31:51,660
brand products are produced to order, so it gives us the

508
00:31:51,661 --> 00:31:55,780
ability to slit and perf in line. And as you can see, there's no

509
00:31:55,781 --> 00:31:58,980
waste during our extrusion process. We trim off the sides,

510
00:31:58,981 --> 00:32:02,420
it goes into a vacuum tube underneath the floor, ends up

511
00:32:02,421 --> 00:32:05,320
back in the extruder, turns back into a resin so we can

512
00:32:05,321 --> 00:32:08,920
We've been instructed more products.

513
00:32:09,020 --> 00:32:11,820
The discards are melted and formed into pellets

514
00:32:11,920 --> 00:32:14,120
that become more bubble wrap.

515
00:32:14,220 --> 00:32:16,760
The same goes for truckloads of used bubble wrap

516
00:32:16,860 --> 00:32:21,060
returned to the factory each week, part of the 32% of garbage

517
00:32:21,160 --> 00:32:25,380
that Americans recycle every year.

518
00:32:25,480 --> 00:32:26,880
A lot of the stuff that you see here now

519
00:32:26,980 --> 00:32:28,920
is returns that customers use it,

520
00:32:29,020 --> 00:32:31,220
and when they're done with it, they bring it back to us.

521
00:32:31,320 --> 00:32:32,620
We reprocess it.

522
00:32:32,720 --> 00:32:35,320
We don't want any of this to go into the landfill.

523
00:32:35,321 --> 00:32:37,760
Each year, sealed air makes enough of this packaging

524
00:32:37,860 --> 00:32:42,620
wonder to wrap around the equator 10 times.

525
00:32:42,720 --> 00:32:47,360
Being mostly air, the huge rolls are light in weight.

526
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:52,140
But the volume of space required to ship and store them is huge.

527
00:32:52,240 --> 00:32:54,940
The answer to this problem is a new type of bubble wrap

528
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:57,440
called New Air IB.

529
00:32:57,540 --> 00:33:01,420
The IB stands for inflatable bubble.

530
00:33:01,520 --> 00:33:03,180
It's our fastest growing product line.

531
00:33:03,280 --> 00:33:05,080
It's a roll of film that the customer blows up

532
00:33:05,081 --> 00:33:06,740
up at their own factory.

533
00:33:06,840 --> 00:33:09,280
This 24 rolls equates to a full truckload of bubble,

534
00:33:09,380 --> 00:33:11,180
which is 70 to 80 bundles of bubble.

535
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:12,680
So it's a real space saver for the customer.

536
00:33:16,660 --> 00:33:18,220
This machine takes on the challenge

537
00:33:18,320 --> 00:33:20,620
of inflating the hundreds of thousands of bubbles

538
00:33:20,720 --> 00:33:23,340
on a single roll.

539
00:33:23,440 --> 00:33:25,740
That doesn't happen until the company doing the packaging

540
00:33:25,840 --> 00:33:28,840
is ready to ship.

541
00:33:28,940 --> 00:33:31,240
There's one key difference between this inflatable bubble

542
00:33:31,340 --> 00:33:34,380
wrap and the original.

543
00:33:34,381 --> 00:33:37,880
All the air pockets on a roll are interconnected, making it

544
00:33:37,980 --> 00:33:42,180
impossible to pop. So if you're looking for stress relief,

545
00:33:42,280 --> 00:33:46,120
stick with the original. But what about packaging fragile

546
00:33:46,220 --> 00:33:48,520
items that need more than a cushion of air to survive

547
00:33:48,620 --> 00:33:54,840
shipping? Sealed air offers foam, but not

548
00:33:54,940 --> 00:33:58,680
just any foam. It's called Instapack, and it's

549
00:33:58,780 --> 00:34:01,840
the result of two separate liquid chemicals that expand

550
00:34:01,841 --> 00:34:07,640
when they're combined. The process can be performed at

551
00:34:07,641 --> 00:34:12,680
shipping sites or even in your home.

552
00:34:12,681 --> 00:34:17,680
Easily activated by simply breaking a seal and mixing the

553
00:34:17,681 --> 00:34:21,800
two components back and forth until the liquid foam starts to

554
00:34:21,801 --> 00:34:25,800
rise. The bag is then placed into the

555
00:34:25,801 --> 00:34:35,940
box, and the product is packaged for shipment.

556
00:34:35,941 --> 00:34:38,640
No matter how effective your package's cushioning material

557
00:34:38,641 --> 00:34:43,360
is, you still need a sturdy container to hold it all.

558
00:34:43,361 --> 00:34:47,160
Enter that indispensable icon of the packaging industry, the

559
00:34:47,161 --> 00:34:50,600
cardboard box. Cardboard was invented by the

560
00:34:50,601 --> 00:34:55,800
Chinese in the 16th century. But it wasn't until 1879 that

561
00:34:55,801 --> 00:34:59,640
a Brooklyn bag maker named Robert Gare had the ingenious idea

562
00:34:59,641 --> 00:35:02,100
that turned it into today's mass-produced packaging

563
00:35:02,101 --> 00:35:09,640
marvel, a die-cut and scored box that could be stored flat and

564
00:35:09,641 --> 00:35:15,320
then easily folded for use. Without it, we'd still be

565
00:35:15,321 --> 00:35:18,280
shipping most products in wooden crates, and there'd be no

566
00:35:18,281 --> 00:35:25,320
cardboard box industry worth $50 billion.

567
00:35:25,321 --> 00:35:29,560
The Sealed Air Corporation's first major client was IBM,

568
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:34,220
which used bubble wrap to ship its IBM 1401, one of the world's

569
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:36,360
first mass-produced business computers.

570
00:35:43,680 --> 00:35:46,180
Packaging can play a crucial role in settings you've

571
00:35:46,280 --> 00:35:50,880
probably never conceived of, like disaster sites.

572
00:35:50,980 --> 00:35:54,720
Imagine how handy it would be to have a giant impermeable bag

573
00:35:54,721 --> 00:35:57,560
that could store oil-slicked debris in the wake of a major

574
00:35:57,660 --> 00:36:01,760
spill, or sand to create sandbags against rising

575
00:36:01,860 --> 00:36:02,560
floodwaters.

576
00:36:05,860 --> 00:36:08,660
The PacTech Corporation of Clinton, Louisiana,

577
00:36:08,760 --> 00:36:10,860
has answered the challenge.

578
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:15,280
Their mega bags can even safely package radioactive waste, and

579
00:36:15,380 --> 00:36:17,920
have one key advantage over the steel containers used

580
00:36:18,020 --> 00:36:20,820
extensively for decades.

581
00:36:20,920 --> 00:36:22,820
The benefits of flexible packaging

582
00:36:22,821 --> 00:36:24,920
over the standard metal containers

583
00:36:25,020 --> 00:36:27,720
is the space required to store it.

584
00:36:27,820 --> 00:36:29,620
Typically, these projects are very large,

585
00:36:29,720 --> 00:36:32,860
and the number of containers needed are large.

586
00:36:32,960 --> 00:36:34,560
And with the flexible container, we

587
00:36:34,660 --> 00:36:37,260
can package tons of units on pallets.

588
00:36:40,380 --> 00:36:43,400
But how do you make a bag for radioactive waste that's

589
00:36:43,500 --> 00:36:47,480
just as effective as a rigid steel drum?

590
00:36:47,580 --> 00:36:50,920
The secret lies in multiple layers of heavy plastic

591
00:36:50,921 --> 00:36:56,620
and woven fiber materials. We have two main materials.

592
00:36:56,720 --> 00:36:59,760
We use a 6-ounce woven polypropylene, and we also have

593
00:36:59,860 --> 00:37:02,160
the felt material. It's a non-woven.

594
00:37:02,260 --> 00:37:04,260
It's a 12-ounce. It's pretty thick.

595
00:37:04,360 --> 00:37:06,900
Whatever going into the bag, it won't puncture through that

596
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:11,880
12-ounce material. But it's not just the heavy-duty

597
00:37:11,980 --> 00:37:14,480
material that makes the bag such effective packages.

598
00:37:14,481 --> 00:37:21,120
It's also the heavy thread used to stitch the layers together.

599
00:37:23,120 --> 00:37:26,500
Consumer thread is graded at a thickness between 100 to 200

600
00:37:26,501 --> 00:37:29,960
denier, the unit of measurement for the density of fibers.

601
00:37:31,440 --> 00:37:35,660
PacTech thread is 30 times heavier, and the sewing machine

602
00:37:35,661 --> 00:37:38,500
stitching it is truly industrial strength.

603
00:37:39,580 --> 00:37:42,260
Don't get your hands in there because it'll sew anything that

604
00:37:42,261 --> 00:37:42,961
goes through it.

605
00:37:44,480 --> 00:37:48,320
Each bag comes equipped with straps and zippers that make

606
00:37:48,420 --> 00:37:52,280
moving and using it easier. Construction methods ensure that

607
00:37:52,380 --> 00:37:54,520
these features won't leak or rupture.

608
00:37:54,620 --> 00:37:58,520
These straps are actually what is used to lift this bag up,

609
00:37:58,620 --> 00:38:02,540
full of debris, dirt, whatever they're putting into it.

610
00:38:02,640 --> 00:38:05,240
Once they get it, they will fill it up and they'll zip it.

611
00:38:05,340 --> 00:38:07,540
Sometimes they'll put dirt, sometimes they'll put crushed

612
00:38:07,640 --> 00:38:08,740
concrete.

613
00:38:08,840 --> 00:38:10,240
There's no telling what they put in them.

614
00:38:10,241 --> 00:38:13,840
put in them.

615
00:38:13,841 --> 00:38:16,040
To make sure the bags are hefty enough to handle

616
00:38:16,041 --> 00:38:19,880
radioactive waste, Pac-Tec runs tests at its facility.

617
00:38:23,820 --> 00:38:26,960
What they do is typically load those with heavy equipment

618
00:38:26,961 --> 00:38:31,900
into the bags, take the bag out of the loading frame that it's

619
00:38:31,901 --> 00:38:36,340
sitting in, and load them onto flight bed trucks or rail cars

620
00:38:36,341 --> 00:38:39,000
to be transported to disposal sites that are approved for low

621
00:38:39,001 --> 00:38:42,940
radioactive soils and construction debris.

622
00:38:43,040 --> 00:38:45,800
As packaging experts are solving environmental problems

623
00:38:45,900 --> 00:38:49,740
on Earth, they're also pitching in as we reach further into

624
00:38:49,840 --> 00:38:51,940
space.

625
00:38:52,040 --> 00:38:56,760
In 2010, engineers at NASA's Johnson Space Center in Houston

626
00:38:56,860 --> 00:38:58,760
faced a unique challenge.

627
00:38:58,860 --> 00:39:02,960
They had to package a complex $10 million human-like robot for

628
00:39:03,060 --> 00:39:09,000
a rough ride into orbit on the space shuttle.

629
00:39:09,001 --> 00:39:14,300
Robonaut 2, R2 for short, was designed to help astronauts by

630
00:39:14,301 --> 00:39:17,400
performing routine menial tasks on the International Space

631
00:39:17,401 --> 00:39:19,840
Station.

632
00:39:19,841 --> 00:39:24,040
But first he had to get there, and safely.

633
00:39:24,041 --> 00:39:26,860
What we're most concerned about during launch is the vibration

634
00:39:26,861 --> 00:39:27,860
environment.

635
00:39:27,861 --> 00:39:30,860
We've all seen images of the crew in the cockpit of the shuttle

636
00:39:30,861 --> 00:39:31,860
during launch.

637
00:39:31,861 --> 00:39:33,560
They get buffeted about quite a bit.

638
00:39:33,561 --> 00:39:35,460
It's a very dynamic environment.

639
00:39:35,461 --> 00:39:38,760
So it's something that we have to design for and take care of

640
00:39:38,860 --> 00:39:41,160
during that ascent period of launch.

641
00:39:48,200 --> 00:39:52,180
Since Robonaut 2 weighs a hefty 330 pounds,

642
00:39:52,280 --> 00:39:54,340
you might think he's sturdy enough to survive launch

643
00:39:54,440 --> 00:39:56,580
without much help.

644
00:39:56,680 --> 00:39:59,780
But he's also very fragile, particularly

645
00:39:59,880 --> 00:40:01,460
in his extremities.

646
00:40:01,560 --> 00:40:05,460
R2's hands have been produced with unprecedented dexterity.

647
00:40:05,461 --> 00:40:08,660
The sensing capabilities it has at the fingertips can detect

648
00:40:08,760 --> 00:40:11,160
on the order of grams, so you can literally put a quarter

649
00:40:11,260 --> 00:40:15,600
on its finger and it would know that the quarter's there.

650
00:40:15,700 --> 00:40:19,700
Could NASA's packaging experts preserve R2's fragile anatomy

651
00:40:19,800 --> 00:40:23,120
during the critical launch phase?

652
00:40:23,220 --> 00:40:26,420
Initial efforts were disappointing, including one

653
00:40:26,520 --> 00:40:29,920
that bundled R2 in a foam cushion container that failed

654
00:40:30,020 --> 00:40:31,520
to provide enough protection.

655
00:40:31,521 --> 00:40:36,860
protection. The eventual solution came in

656
00:40:36,861 --> 00:40:41,240
the form of a box dubbed Sleeper, short for Structural Launch

657
00:40:41,241 --> 00:40:46,100
Enclosure to Effectively Protect Robonaut.

658
00:40:46,101 --> 00:40:48,740
NASA chronicled its construction with a time-lapse camera.

659
00:40:48,741 --> 00:40:51,580
What they have on the table at the moment is the base plate

660
00:40:51,581 --> 00:40:53,580
of our Sleeper box. We start out with just that

661
00:40:53,581 --> 00:40:56,340
one plate. Struts were then

662
00:40:56,341 --> 00:40:58,920
positioned to support R2's back and shoulders.

663
00:40:58,921 --> 00:41:01,420
At this point, he's fully attached and self-supported.

664
00:41:01,520 --> 00:41:03,020
They attach the brackets to Robonaut.

665
00:41:03,120 --> 00:41:05,220
That's the first step after he attaches to the base plate.

666
00:41:05,320 --> 00:41:07,120
The next thing they do is they put on the rear panel.

667
00:41:11,060 --> 00:41:13,460
Dense foam provided more support,

668
00:41:13,560 --> 00:41:15,360
followed by an aluminum frame.

669
00:41:15,460 --> 00:41:17,880
Now you have a completely enclosed sleeper.

670
00:41:17,980 --> 00:41:18,880
It's got foam inside.

671
00:41:18,980 --> 00:41:20,380
It's got foam outside.

672
00:41:20,480 --> 00:41:22,280
It's got a little straps that hold the foam together.

673
00:41:22,380 --> 00:41:26,280
So he is completely now enclosed and protected.

674
00:41:26,380 --> 00:41:28,920
Since R2 is almost human,

675
00:41:28,921 --> 00:41:31,620
It's only fitting that his position during launch will be

676
00:41:31,720 --> 00:41:36,360
flat on his back, just like the astronauts on the flight deck.

677
00:41:36,460 --> 00:41:38,860
And if you're wondering what this prized package can do

678
00:41:38,960 --> 00:41:43,760
aboard the International Space Station, here's a sample.

679
00:41:43,860 --> 00:41:45,940
I'm going to show you a case where the robot is working

680
00:41:46,040 --> 00:41:47,480
with thermal blankets.

681
00:41:47,580 --> 00:41:48,980
To be able to work with something like this,

682
00:41:49,080 --> 00:41:50,880
you have to have the flexibility you do with, in this case,

683
00:41:50,980 --> 00:41:53,280
human-like hands.

684
00:41:53,380 --> 00:41:54,680
After pulling back the space blanket,

685
00:41:54,780 --> 00:41:56,980
which is something it would do to help the crew in terms of

686
00:41:56,981 --> 00:41:58,220
of setting up a work site.

687
00:41:58,320 --> 00:42:00,020
We're also going to work with another flexible item, something

688
00:42:00,120 --> 00:42:01,720
you don't normally see robots working with.

689
00:42:01,820 --> 00:42:02,980
In this case, it's an envelope, and it's

690
00:42:03,080 --> 00:42:03,980
going to hand it to me.

691
00:42:07,220 --> 00:42:09,720
Thank you, Robonaut.

692
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:12,720
Chalk up the packing of Robonaut among packaging's

693
00:42:12,820 --> 00:42:14,640
countless successes.

694
00:42:14,740 --> 00:42:19,240
And to borrow a phrase from that world, as well as Hollywood's,

695
00:42:19,340 --> 00:42:20,140
that's a wrap.

